{
 "_note": "Atlas text record id -> Library id (or a list of Library ids when one record is covered by several files). Checked by hand against the Library title and the text itself, for records whose titles differ from the Library's so the title matcher cannot see them. Where a record is only partly covered, the Library entry says so (see TEXT_AUDIT.md). Not listed: records that merely share words with a Library title (Genesis Rabbah is not Genesis; the Book of Abraham is not the Apocalypse of Abraham). \"_partial\" lists the records where the file holds only part of the work. \"_exclude\" lists records the title matcher pairs with a Library entry that does not hold them (the Orphic gold tablets are not in the Orphic Hymns file). The Heart and Diamond Sutra files held the Buddhacarita until the audit; they now hold the sutras (Müller, 1894). Later additions (16 November 2026): the Puranas, Dharmasutras, Manu, Vedanta and other darshana sutras, the Pali chronicles and commentaries, the second and third English Qur’ans, Pahlavi books, and the second translations of the Digha, Majjhima, Vinaya, Lotus and Yoga texts, so that one Atlas record can open several Library files. Further additions (16 November 2026, second round): Egyptian, Mesopotamian and Persian texts, Jewish, Islamic, Bahá’í and Indic works, Buddhist, Jain and Chinese works, Christian classics and creeds, and texts of the Americas and Hawaii, for the records whose titles differ from the Library’s (checked against the text before each was added). Third round (16 November 2026): the Latter-day Saint, Christian Science, Adventist, Quaker and Rastafari books, medieval and Reformation Christian writings, Jewish classics, Sufi texts and others, again checked against the text. Fourth round (16 November 2026): the second sweep of the Atlas for traditions with nothing readable (Latter-day Saint, Jehovah’s Witness, Samaritan, Paulician, Comte, Paine and Schleiermacher, the Marathi and Tamil saints, Welsh and Norse sources, Iroquois and Navajo ceremonies and others). Round 12 additions (16 November 2026): Herodotus Book IV, Tacitus Germania, Suhrawardi’s ʿAwārif, the Racovian Catechism, the Westminster Confession, Elpis Israel, Oahspe, In Darkest England, the Aquarian Gospel, Dhalla’s Zoroastrian Theology and the Ain-i Akbari (volumes I and III). The Unaipon record is excluded: the title matcher paired it with Parker’s Australian Legendary Tales, a different book (Unaipon’s are of 1924–29 and not shown to be open). Round 24 (18 November 2026, the Atlas panel session): records of works the Library does hold under another title, found by comparing every Library text nothing in the Atlas pointed at with the records that had no match: the Rosicrucian manifestos, Ludlul, the Walam Olum, the Ghost-Dance Religion, the Mahatma Letters, Eusebius, the Miscellanies of Clement, Pseudo-Dionysius, the Vision of Paul and the Diatessaron in Ante-Nicene Fathers 9, the Apologies of Justin, the Arabic Infancy Gospel, Dee’s True and Faithful Relation, Caesar’s Gallic War (book VI is in it), and more; each checked against the Library file’s own title and note. The Divine Comedy and Part One of the Pilgrim’s Progress open the imagined shelf’s texts, and the Atlas panel labels them Imagined.",
